WEBVTT

00:00:00.014 --> 00:00:02.947
We will reuse these variables throughout the module.

00:00:02.947 --> 00:00:05.247
Patient ID is an identifier.

00:00:05.247 --> 00:00:07.892
Triage level is an ordered category.

00:00:07.892 --> 00:00:11.882
Temperature is a measurement. Emergency visits is a count.

00:00:11.882 --> 00:00:16.732
Device payload is nested JSON, and a clinical note is free text.

00:00:16.732 --> 00:00:19.172
Notice that the questions are different.

00:00:19.172 --> 00:00:24.242
Some questions are about dataset organization.

00:00:24.242 --> 00:00:26.652
Others are about measurement meaning.

00:00:26.652 --> 00:00:29.947
Others are about numeric behavior.

00:00:29.947 --> 00:00:36.002
Learning to identify which question you are answering is one of the most important skills in this module.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:40.542
Connect this idea to a hospital combining patient tables, device JSON, and clinical notes.

00:00:40.542 --> 00:00:47.882
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:47.882 --> 00:00:53.697
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:53.697 --> 00:00:59.622
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:59.622 --> 00:01:05.440
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
